;; T0.2 negative control — RTO (G4).
;;
;; t=1.1s..2.2s  12 writes complete :ok -> quiet window #1 [1.1s, 3.0s) judged,
;;               ratio 1.00 -> availability passes
;; t=3.0s        :pause      — disruption starts
;; t=4.0s        :pause-stop — quiet window #2 opens: [4.0s, t-end]
;; t=304.0s..   12 writes complete :ok -> availability passes again
;;
;; RTO for the t=4.0s stop = (304.0 - 4.0) = 300s > the :kill/:pause budget of
;; 120s (jepsen/docs/1.md s5.4-2) -> :valid? false with {:type :rto-exceeded}.
;; The trailing write *invoke* at t=303s is what makes the disruption
;; measurable: `gates/rto` only judges a disruption whose stop precedes the
;; last write invoke (the write phase must still have been live, otherwise
;; "no :ok write after the stop" is structural, not a failure).
;; Values are all distinct, so the premise gate passes; the fixture isolates
;; the RTO gate.
[;; ns: history.edn `:time` is a monotonic nanosecond clock on the control node
